/*!
@defgroup tmu
@ingroup mod_system Trigger Mux Unit (TMU)
@brief Configurable trigger-routing matrix for YTMicro peripherals.

@details
The TMU module connects device-specific trigger sources to configurable target
modules such as TMU output pins and peripheral trigger inputs. Each target
selects one source from a device-dependent list, allowing software to build
trigger paths between timers, comparators, ADCs, communication peripherals,
and external TMU inputs.

The SDK exposes the TMU through a two-layer design:
- The **driver layer** (`tmu_driver.h/.c`) provides instance-based APIs for
  batch initialization, single-route updates, and route readback.
- The **hardware access layer** (`tmu_hw_access.h/.c`) provides direct register
  helpers for reset, route programming, route readback, and shared lock-bit
  control.

@section tmu_features Key Features
- Routes one trigger source to each TMU target module.
- Supports batch route programming through `tmu_user_config_t`.
- Supports runtime reconfiguration of individual target modules.
- Supports route readback for software verification and diagnostics.
- Exposes register-level lock control through the HW access layer.
- Uses device-specific trigger source and target enumerations generated in the
  active `<device>_features.h` file.
- Supports instance-based access through `TMU_DRV_*` APIs.

@section tmu_modes Supported Modes

| Mode | APIs | Description |
|------|------|-------------|
| Batch routing initialization | TMU_DRV_Init() | Clears the TMU matrix, then applies an array of source-to-target mappings. |
| Runtime single-target update | TMU_DRV_SetTrigSourceForTargetModule() | Changes the source assigned to one target module. |
| Route readback | TMU_DRV_GetTrigSourceForTargetModule() | Reads the source currently selected for one target module. |
| Register reset | TMU_DRV_Deinit(), TMU_Init() | Writes reset values to all TMU MUX registers. |
| Lock control (internal layer) | TMU_SetLockForTargetModule(), TMU_GetLockForTargetModule() | Sets or reads the shared lock bit for the MUX register that owns a target module. |

@section tmu_arch Module Architecture

The TMU module is organized in a two-layer architecture:

@verbatim
+----------------------------------------------+
|              Application Code                |
|         (calls TMU_DRV_* functions)          |
+----------------------------------------------+
|          TMU Driver Layer (tmu_driver.h/c)   |
|  - Instance-based API (TMU_DRV_Init, etc.)   |
|  - Applies user-defined mapping arrays       |
|  - Resolves base address through TMU_BASE_PTRS |
+----------------------------------------------+
|     TMU HW Access Layer (tmu_hw_access.h/c)  |
|  - Direct MUX register access                |
|  - Route programming / readback              |
|  - Shared lock-bit control                   |
+----------------------------------------------+
|             TMU Peripheral Hardware          |
|        (MUX[n] registers, SELx, LOCK)        |
+----------------------------------------------+
@endverbatim

@subsection tmu_arch_driver Driver Layer (tmu_driver.h)
Application code should normally use the `TMU_DRV_*` functions. The driver
accepts a zero-based instance index, resolves the TMU base address internally,
and applies route mappings described by `tmu_user_config_t`.

@subsection tmu_arch_hw HW Access Layer (tmu_hw_access.h)
This internal layer provides direct register operations for:
- **Initialization** - TMU_Init()
- **Routing Configuration** - TMU_SetTrigSourceForTargetModule()
- **Routing Query** - TMU_GetTrigSourceForTargetModule()
- **Lock Control** - TMU_SetLockForTargetModule(), TMU_GetLockForTargetModule()

@section tmu_config Configuration Parameters

@subsection tmu_config_mapping tmu_inout_mapping_config_t Fields
| Field | Type | Description | Default |
|-------|------|-------------|---------|
| triggerSource | tmu_trigger_source_t | Trigger source that will drive the route. | User supplied |
| targetModule | tmu_target_module_t | Target module that receives the selected source. | User supplied |

@subsection tmu_config_user tmu_user_config_t Fields
| Field | Type | Description | Default |
|-------|------|-------------|---------|
| numInOutMappingConfigs | uint8_t | Number of valid entries in `inOutMappingConfig`. | 0 |
| inOutMappingConfig | const tmu_inout_mapping_config_t * | Pointer to the mapping array consumed by TMU_DRV_Init(). | NULL |

@subsection tmu_config_features Device Definitions
| Definition | Description |
|------------|-------------|
| TMU_INSTANCE_COUNT | Number of TMU peripheral instances available on the device. |
| TMU_MUX_COUNT | Number of TMU MUX registers implemented by the device. |
| TMU_MUX_SEL0_WIDTH | Width of each source-selection field; used to validate trigger source values. |
| TMU_BASE_PTRS | Peripheral base-address table used by the driver layer. |
| FEATURE_TMU_TARGET_MODULE | Device-specific list of supported TMU target modules. |
| tmu_trigger_source_e | Device-generated enumeration containing all valid trigger sources. |
| tmu_target_module_e | Device-generated enumeration containing all valid target modules. |

@section tmu_usage Usage Patterns

@subsection tmu_usage_basic Pattern 1: Batch Initialization With Two Routes
Use `tmu_user_config_t` to reset the TMU matrix and then program multiple
routes in one call.

@code{.c}
#define INST_TMU (0U)

static const tmu_inout_mapping_config_t tmuRoutes[] =
{
    { TMU_TRIG_SOURCE_TMU_IN0, TMU_TARGET_MODULE_TMU_OUT0 },
    { TMU_TRIG_SOURCE_TMU_IN1, TMU_TARGET_MODULE_TMU_OUT1 }
};

tmu_user_config_t tmuConfig =
{
    .numInOutMappingConfigs = (uint8_t)(sizeof(tmuRoutes) / sizeof(tmuRoutes[0])),
    .inOutMappingConfig = tmuRoutes
};

/* Enable the TMU peripheral clock before this call. */
TMU_DRV_Init(INST_TMU, &tmuConfig);
@endcode

@subsection tmu_usage_update Pattern 2: Runtime Route Update and Readback
Update one target module at runtime and read back the selected source.

@code{.c}
#define INST_TMU (0U)

tmu_trigger_source_t activeSource;

TMU_DRV_SetTrigSourceForTargetModule(INST_TMU,
                                     TMU_TRIG_SOURCE_TMU_IN1,
                                     TMU_TARGET_MODULE_TMU_OUT0);

activeSource = TMU_DRV_GetTrigSourceForTargetModule(INST_TMU,
                                                    TMU_TARGET_MODULE_TMU_OUT0);
@endcode

@subsection tmu_usage_lock Pattern 3: Lock a Shared MUX Register Through HW Access
Use the internal HW access layer when software must set or inspect the TMU
lock bit directly.

@code{.c}
#define INST_TMU (0U)

static TMU_Type *const s_tmuBase[] = TMU_BASE_PTRS;
TMU_Type *base = s_tmuBase[INST_TMU];
bool locked;

TMU_SetLockForTargetModule(base, TMU_TARGET_MODULE_TMU_OUT0);

/* TMU_OUT0 and TMU_OUT1 may share the same MUX register on the device. */
locked = TMU_GetLockForTargetModule(base, TMU_TARGET_MODULE_TMU_OUT1);
@endcode

@subsection tmu_usage_reset Pattern 4: Restore Reset Routing
Reset every TMU route to its register reset value.

@code{.c}
#define INST_TMU (0U)

TMU_DRV_Deinit(INST_TMU);
@endcode

@section tmu_api API Quick Reference

@subsection tmu_api_driver Driver Layer

@subsubsection tmu_api_driver_init Initialization & De-initialization
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| TMU_DRV_Init() | Reset the TMU matrix and apply a user-defined mapping array. |
| TMU_DRV_Deinit() | Restore all TMU MUX registers to reset values. |

@subsubsection tmu_api_driver_config Routing Configuration
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| TMU_DRV_SetTrigSourceForTargetModule() | Change the source assigned to one target module. |

@subsubsection tmu_api_driver_query Routing Query
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| TMU_DRV_GetTrigSourceForTargetModule() | Read back the source currently assigned to one target module. |

@subsection tmu_api_hw HW Access Layer

@subsubsection tmu_api_hw_init Initialization
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| TMU_Init() | Issue reset-value writes to all TMU MUX registers. |

@subsubsection tmu_api_hw_config Routing Configuration
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| TMU_SetTrigSourceForTargetModule() | Program one TMU source-selection field directly. |

@subsubsection tmu_api_hw_query Routing Query
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| TMU_GetTrigSourceForTargetModule() | Read one TMU source-selection field directly. |

@subsubsection tmu_api_hw_lock Lock Control
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| TMU_SetLockForTargetModule() | Set the shared lock bit of the owning TMU MUX register. |
| TMU_GetLockForTargetModule() | Read the shared lock bit of the owning TMU MUX register. |

@section tmu_notes Important Notes

@subsection tmu_notes_clock Clock Dependency
The TMU driver does not enable clocks internally. The TMU peripheral clock must
be enabled by the clock manager before calling TMU_DRV_Init(),
TMU_DRV_Deinit(), or any HW access function.

@subsection tmu_notes_device Device-Specific Enumerations
The valid values of `tmu_trigger_source_t` and `tmu_target_module_t` depend on
the selected device. Always consult the active `<device>_features.h` file when
choosing source and target constants.

@subsection tmu_notes_lock Shared Register Lock Behavior
The TMU lock bit is stored per MUX register, while each register contains four
target selections. Locking one target therefore affects the other three target
slots that share the same register.

@warning After a TMU MUX register is locked, later route updates issued through
TMU_DRV_SetTrigSourceForTargetModule() or TMU_SetTrigSourceForTargetModule()
may be ignored by hardware.

@subsection tmu_notes_return Driver Return Behavior
The current TMU driver implementation returns `STATUS_SUCCESS` after issuing
reset or route-write requests. It does not perform a software readback or
explicit lock-state check before returning.

@subsection tmu_notes_config Mapping Array Constraints
If `numInOutMappingConfigs` is non-zero, `inOutMappingConfig` must point to a
valid array of route descriptors. Passing a NULL pointer with a non-zero count
causes invalid memory access in the driver loop.

@section tmu_integration Integration Guide

@subsection tmu_integration_units Compilation Units
Compile the following files into the application:

@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/tmu/tmu_driver.c
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/tmu/tmu_hw_access.c
@endverbatim

@subsection tmu_integration_include Include Path
Add the driver include directory to the toolchain include path:

@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/inc/
@endverbatim

@subsection tmu_integration_symbols Compile Symbols
No special compile symbols are required for the TMU driver itself. The active
device selection must provide the matching `device_registers.h` and
`<device>_features.h` definitions.

@subsection tmu_integration_dependencies Dependencies
- Clock manager configuration for enabling the TMU peripheral clock
- Device register and feature headers selected by the build target
- `tmu_hw_access.h` only if the application uses direct lock control or other
  internal-layer helpers
*/
